REM RESTORE: from the RISC OS BBC BASIC manual, keywords chapter
5 DATA 1,2
10 READ a,b:PRINT a;" ";b
20 RESTORE:READ a:PRINT "RESTORE: ";a
30 RESTORE 1000:READ a$:PRINT "RESTORE 1000: ";a$
50 n%=1010:RESTORE n%:READ a$:PRINT "RESTORE n%: ";a$
55 REM (Z80: +0 and +1 both mean the next line; BASIC V: +1 is the one after)
60 RESTORE +1:READ a$:PRINT "RESTORE +1: ";a$
70 DATA plus zero
80 DATA plus one
90 RESTORE +0:READ a$:PRINT "RESTORE +0: ";a$:RESTORE +2:READ a$:PRINT "RESTORE +2: ";a$
100 DATA next line
105 DATA two lines on
110 RESTORE 1000:READ a$:PRINT "main: ";a$
120 PROCread:READ a$:PRINT "main again: ";a$
130 ON ERROR LOCAL PRINT ERR;" ";REPORT$:GOTO 150
135 REM (the Z80 version needs the line to exist; BASIC V uses the next one)
140 RESTORE 995
150 REM (RESTORE DATA with no LOCAL DATA: test/diff/d13restoredata)
170 ON ERROR PRINT ERR;" ";REPORT$:END
180 RESTORE 1010:READ a$,b$,c$
200 DEF PROCread
210 LOCAL DATA
220 RESTORE 1010:READ a$:PRINT "in PROC: ";a$
230 RESTORE DATA
240 ENDPROC
1000 DATA line 1000
1010 DATA line 1010,last
RUN
NEW
